# Decide the Observational Memory Repository Boundary

## Step

Decide whether the Effect Schema observational projection becomes a new focused repository, remains inside `joelclaw`, or uses a split where a new core repo owns domain/ports/machines and `joelclaw` owns runtime adapters. Use `/Users/joel/Code/joelhooks/content-grill` as the SOTA new-repo reference: pnpm workspaces + Turbo, Effect v4 domain schemas and `Context.Service` ports, XState v5 lifecycle machines, strict TypeScript 7, Oxlint/Ultracite/Oxfmt, Lefthook gates, vendored Effect/XState sources, and thin CLI composition roots. Resolve package boundaries, repository ownership, release/adapter coupling, and where the grill-master brief moves if ownership changes.

## Result

Closed. The semantic core lives in the new private repository [`joelhooks/joelclaw-memory`](https://github.com/joelhooks/joelclaw-memory). It owns proposed Effect Schema contracts, evidence and privacy rules, migrations, atomic projection-pass boundaries, search contracts, and invoked XState lifecycle machines.

`joelclaw` remains the accepted-Run capture, storage, event, and compatibility adapter. `joelclaw-observer` remains the host/Pi adapter until a deliberate migration replaces it. `content-grill` and the local `ts-cli-template` were observed as references; neither became a dependency or copy source.

The initial contract scaffold is committed as `0a4c73af5dc84170c70ef5c51ba812165cd2c2b2`. It is explicitly not authorized for live adapters or production search replacement. The schema-settlement and shadow-prototype steps remain the gates.
